WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.921
- Marcia said that she was who she was

00:00:02.921 --> 00:00:06.040
and her motto was, "I'm me, I'm here,

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.290
"and you all better get your life."

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.790
(jazzy music)

00:00:26.503 --> 00:00:27.985
- A lot of people that comment on Basquiat

00:00:27.985 --> 00:00:30.247
say that the pieces are
like, a child could draw that

00:00:30.247 --> 00:00:33.064
and they're not painted by
someone who's an artist,

00:00:33.064 --> 00:00:35.180
but he's an extremely talented painter.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.402
Although the drawings look simple,

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:37.902
they were extremely complex.

00:00:37.902 --> 00:00:40.818
They usually talked about social
issues, social commentary.

00:00:40.818 --> 00:00:42.890
They dealt with things such as racism,

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.732
economic equality, and all
these things in the nation.

00:00:45.732 --> 00:00:47.502
As an artist, he's influenced me

00:00:47.502 --> 00:00:49.982
because he was someone
that was very brash.

00:00:49.982 --> 00:00:52.736
His look was very
uncaring, but it was helm.

00:00:52.736 --> 00:00:54.346
And that's something
that's been influencing me,

00:00:54.346 --> 00:00:55.935
especially with growing out my hair.

00:00:55.935 --> 00:00:58.615
I saw him as someone who
was extremely comfortable

00:00:58.615 --> 00:01:00.681
in who he was and the way that he looked

00:01:00.681 --> 00:01:02.639
was exactly how he wanted to look.

00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:04.429
For as long as he lived,
that's how society thought

00:01:04.429 --> 00:01:06.165
that a black man's hair should be.

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:08.241
At that time, (mumbling)
wearing these clothes.

00:01:08.241 --> 00:01:09.812
Looking at the photo while
I was trying to do it

00:01:09.812 --> 00:01:11.733
was really hard, and then
to see the finished product

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:13.570
was like, "Oh, I accomplished it."

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:14.804
It was really cool to see.

00:01:14.804 --> 00:01:15.861
It looked like who I look up to.

00:01:15.861 --> 00:01:18.278
It was a really good feeling.

00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:24.635
- There was the Civil Rights
movement, and then Malcolm X.

00:01:24.635 --> 00:01:27.603
I think a lot of people
misinterpreted him.

00:01:27.603 --> 00:01:30.250
A lot of people consider him
to be the one who was like,

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.920
"No, there needs to be violence,"

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.187
when actually, he was the one saying,

00:01:33.187 --> 00:01:35.506
"No, there needs to be
peaceful protesting,

00:01:35.506 --> 00:01:36.865
"but black people also need to learn

00:01:36.865 --> 00:01:38.246
"how to defend themselves."

00:01:38.246 --> 00:01:41.933
He was just very realistic,
very unapologetic.

00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:44.079
With segregation, when
segregation was happening,

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:47.552
he was also very much like,
"No, okay, this is happening,

00:01:47.552 --> 00:01:50.061
"but we need to also
have black organizations.

00:01:50.061 --> 00:01:53.136
"We need to organize
black people together.

00:01:53.136 --> 00:01:55.397
"If they want segregation, then
we're gonna give it to them

00:01:55.397 --> 00:01:57.842
"and prove to them that black people

00:01:57.842 --> 00:01:59.736
"can still flourish throughout this time."

00:01:59.736 --> 00:02:01.141
Just because the system is against you,

00:02:01.141 --> 00:02:03.107
doesn't mean that you have
to be against yourself.

00:02:03.107 --> 00:02:06.035
You can still fight and
you can still make it.

00:02:06.035 --> 00:02:09.193
That's important, because these
issues that were happening

00:02:09.193 --> 00:02:11.960
in the '60s and '70s are
still happening today.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.149
I mean, yes, we have
made a lot of progress,

00:02:15.149 --> 00:02:17.686
but there's still a huge
fight that needs to happen.

00:02:17.686 --> 00:02:21.804
I was almost moved to tears,
which was really surprising.

00:02:21.804 --> 00:02:25.164
I didn't expect to be
so moved to see myself

00:02:25.164 --> 00:02:27.539
with a side by side picture of him

00:02:27.539 --> 00:02:29.600
and kind of look like him.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.267
It surprised me, I
actually did really well.

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:40.072
- James Baldwin was a writer poet.

00:02:40.072 --> 00:02:43.887
He specialized in thinking
and writing and critiquing

00:02:43.887 --> 00:02:46.046
on black culture or the black experience.

00:02:46.046 --> 00:02:47.416
I think it's important for the world,

00:02:47.416 --> 00:02:49.450
and not just Americans
even, the entire world,

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.931
because they need to
know that black people,

00:02:51.931 --> 00:02:55.168
or people of color artists
are here and existing,

00:02:55.168 --> 00:02:57.525
and have something to say, and are valued.

00:02:57.525 --> 00:02:59.851
He makes me feel okay that I walk away

00:02:59.851 --> 00:03:01.637
for something that feels like a good deal

00:03:01.637 --> 00:03:04.453
or feels like a good job
if I'm unhappy with it.

00:03:04.453 --> 00:03:06.161
He talks about the good
deal or the good job

00:03:06.161 --> 00:03:08.757
as this warm house, and yet
you can't get inside of it

00:03:08.757 --> 00:03:10.391
and you don't want to be inside of it.

00:03:10.391 --> 00:03:12.531
If you get inside of it, you
will leave after five minutes

00:03:12.531 --> 00:03:15.231
because you just have to be
working on your own thing.

00:03:15.231 --> 00:03:18.302
It felt great to talk and
take photos and be Baldwin.

00:03:18.302 --> 00:03:20.234
I think he was important
to the black community

00:03:20.234 --> 00:03:22.005
to know that it's been done for,

00:03:22.005 --> 00:03:24.422
and that you can do it again.

00:03:26.701 --> 00:03:28.970
- During a time when women were like,

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.660
girding their loins and
hiding their bodies,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.870
she was celebrating her
body dancing topless

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.325
and had over a thousand
marriage proposals.

00:03:37.325 --> 00:03:38.998
Growing up and hearing
about Josephine Baker

00:03:38.998 --> 00:03:41.237
and how in control of
her sexuality she was,

00:03:41.237 --> 00:03:43.325
it was definitely
something that I looked to

00:03:43.325 --> 00:03:44.994
when I became a woman.

00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:48.060
During World War II, Josephine
Baker worked with the French

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.521
to carry secrets in their sheet music

00:03:50.521 --> 00:03:54.421
and was awarded multiple French
medals of honor afterwards.

00:03:54.421 --> 00:03:55.676
She came back to the United States

00:03:55.676 --> 00:03:57.015
during the Civil Rights movement,

00:03:57.015 --> 00:03:58.809
was the only woman to speak

00:03:58.809 --> 00:04:01.629
during the 1963 March on Washington.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:04.173
Not only was she a celebrity,
but she parlayed her celebrity

00:04:04.173 --> 00:04:06.847
into major activism and used her power

00:04:06.847 --> 00:04:09.680
to the betterment of other people.

00:04:12.346 --> 00:04:15.314
- Marsha P. Johnson was a fierce activist

00:04:15.314 --> 00:04:18.875
and advocate from the
late '60s and early '70s.

00:04:18.875 --> 00:04:20.286
Let me paint you a picture.

00:04:20.286 --> 00:04:23.546
It's 1969, you're black, you're a woman,

00:04:23.546 --> 00:04:26.770
and you're a member of
the queer community.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:29.580
She realized that
equality was for everyone,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.616
despite your sexuality, your
orientation or your race.

00:04:33.616 --> 00:04:37.684
She was a part of the major
movement in the Stonewall riots

00:04:37.684 --> 00:04:41.767
in 1969, and the unfortunate
part is, after all the work

00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:45.710
that her and Sylvia Rivera
did for the gay movement,

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.903
they actually shunned
her and they despised her

00:04:47.903 --> 00:04:50.618
because she didn't fit
into a certain class.

00:04:50.618 --> 00:04:52.843
Stonewall was a huge part of the movement

00:04:52.843 --> 00:04:54.495
because they weren't afraid.

00:04:54.495 --> 00:04:56.932
It was one of the few times in history

00:04:56.932 --> 00:05:00.195
where queer, trans, gay, lesbian people

00:05:00.195 --> 00:05:02.922
stood together unified
and said that they would

00:05:02.922 --> 00:05:06.271
no longer take it and that
they had a right to hold space.

00:05:06.271 --> 00:05:10.130
It feels good to know that
the reason why I'm here today

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.418
is because of people
like Marsha P. Johnson

00:05:12.418 --> 00:05:13.631
and the work that she's done,

00:05:13.631 --> 00:05:16.994
so I feel I'm a descendant of greatness.

00:05:16.994 --> 00:05:19.269
- It's extremely important
for black children

00:05:19.269 --> 00:05:20.894
to see black heroes in their field,

00:05:20.894 --> 00:05:23.446
because you wanna believe
that you can achieve success.

00:05:23.446 --> 00:05:25.484
You wanna believe that
someone that looks like you,

00:05:25.484 --> 00:05:27.461
went through the same thing as you,

00:05:27.461 --> 00:05:29.149
had the same struggles as you,

00:05:29.149 --> 00:05:30.508
and understands where you're coming from.

00:05:30.508 --> 00:05:32.573
A lot of times, you just want to be happy

00:05:32.573 --> 00:05:33.876
to know that you can do it.

